# Layer 5 — filter-only hash list (EXAMPLE; copy to hash_filter.txt).
#
# One eD2k hash (32 hex chars) per line. A ';' starts an inline comment, so the
# name that justified the entry can be kept next to it — do that, it is the only
# record of why the hash is here.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE
#   Decoys. One hash advertised under many unrelated names: a 700 MB file that
#   claims at once to be a Beatles compilation, Pulp Fiction and an Office
#   installer. Such a file is junk and must not enter the index.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT
#   Child sexual abuse material. That goes in the file named by
#   `content_filter.hash_blocklists` and nowhere else.
#
# WHY THE LISTS ARE SEPARATE
#   A hash here is blocked but carries NO accusation: it does not raise the
#   publisher's csam_attempts and does not count toward
#   publisher_attempt_disconnect_threshold. Someone offering a decoy downloaded
#   it like anyone else, and someone sharing a film is not a criminal.
#
#   Keeping such hashes in the ban list did two kinds of damage — it pushed
#   ordinary users toward a ban, and it diluted a list whose whole value is that
#   every entry means one specific thing. The ban list gets shared with other
#   operators, with its provenance stated; this one should not be.
#
# Hot-reloaded within ~30 s of an mtime change. Copy with `cp` and NOT `cp -p`,
# or the mtime does not change and nothing reloads.
#
# 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef ; 700 MB, 12 names, "Ben E. King - Stand By Me.mp3" + 11 unrelated
